<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Task 6d hardened-runtime entitlements — deliberately non-sandboxed.

  Per docs/plans/native-macos-migration.md's Global Constraints ("The
  initial direct-download build is not App Sandbox enabled") and the
  Task 6d brief's explicit instruction, this file omits
  `com.apple.security.app-sandbox` entirely — its *absence* is the
  intentional statement "this app is not sandboxed," not an oversight.

  Everything else in this dict is scoped to what OmniAgent actually does
  today, checked against the source (macos/OmniAgent/*.swift) rather than
  copied from a template:

    - Local Unix-domain-socket IPC to the PTY daemon (SessionConnection.swift,
      DaemonServiceRegistrar.swift's DaemonSocketProbe) — a plain AF_UNIX
      socket, which needs no entitlement outside App Sandbox.
    - Spawning the bundled, same-identity-signed omniagent-pty-daemon helper
      in degraded app-owned mode (DaemonServiceRegistrar.swift's
      LiveDaemonProcessLauncher, a plain Foundation.Process fork/exec) —
      likewise unrestricted for a non-sandboxed, hardened-runtime app; this
      is the same mechanism countless Mac dev tools use to shell out to
      bundled helpers, and Hardened Runtime's library-validation protections
      apply to code loaded into *this* process's address space (dylibs/
      plugins), not to a separately signed child process.
    - No JIT, no unsigned executable memory, no DYLD environment variables,
      no dynamically loaded unsigned/third-party plugins, and no
      `com.apple.security.get-task-allow` (that entitlement must stay absent
      for a notarizable release build — its presence is a debug-only
      concept and would make `codesign`'s hardened-runtime signature
      reject notarization).

  Net result: no entitlement keys are currently justified beyond the
  explicit non-sandbox statement above. If a future feature needs one
  (e.g. network sockets if the app ever talks to a remote host directly,
  or Apple Events automation), add it here with the same
  "what specifically needs it" justification — not preemptively.
-->
<!--
  Sign in with Apple (future, deliberately NOT enabled): the native login
  screen wires the full ASAuthorization flow, but the entitlement below is
  a *restricted* one — signing with it while the embedded provisioning
  profile doesn't grant it makes the app fail to launch outright
  (taskgated kills it at exec). Until a Developer ID provisioning profile
  carrying it exists, the flow surfaces "Sign in with Apple isn't enabled
  in this build yet" (ASAuthorizationError 1000) and the key stays out:

    <key>com.apple.developer.applesignin</key>
    <array>
      <string>Default</string>
    </array>

  Add it only together with that provisioning profile — never on its own.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict/>
</plist>
